  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale\n- We aim to improve the Erd\u0151s minimum overlap constant by using a patch-based optimization approach combined with multi-resolution analysis and multiple-step tuning.\n- The key bottleneck in the previous approach was its reliance on single-resolution optimization with SLSQP, which tends to get trapped in local optima. Our new approach breaks the problem into three main stages: \n  1) Refined patch selection at 280-point base resolution,\n  2) Multi-resolution optimization using interpolated solutions, and\n  3) Parameter tuning to further refine the solution.\n- This approach combines the strengths of greedy selection with constrained optimization and uses interpolation to effectively transfer solutions between resolutions, providing a more holistic search than local optimization alone.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification\n- Representation: Piecewise constant step function represented by a 280-point vector (or interpolated to 560 points)\n- Objective: Minimize C5 = max_k \u222b|h(x)(1-h(x+k))|dx computed via `np.correlate(..., mode='full') * (2.0/n_points)`\n- Constraints: \n  1) 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all i\n  2) \u2211h[i] = n_points/2 (with dx=2.0/n_points)\n- Objective evaluation: Correlation between h and (1-h) scaled by dx\n- Multi-resolution interpolation: Coarse-to-fine solution transfer using linear interpolation while preserving functional shape\n\n3. Implementation Procedure\n- Validate input `initial_h_values` (280 points) if provided\n- Run primary optimization at 280 points with multi-start using SLSQP \n- Interpolate the best solution to 560 points for finer-grained optimization \n- Perform secondary optimization at 560 points with parameter-tuning \n- Compare solutions from all stages and return the best found\n- Helper functions:\n  - `evaluate_objective(h, n_points)`\n  - `interpolate_solution(h_coarse, n_refinements)`\n  - `objective(h_clip, mode='full')` with clipping\n\n4. Search Choices and Useful Variations\n- Primary stage (280 points):\n  - Start with `initial_h_values` from parent solution\n  - Perform 10 random variations with SLSQP (max_iter=200)\n  - Budget allocation: 20 seconds total for 280-point optimization\n- Secondary stage (560 points):\n  - Start with interpolated 280-point solution\n  - Perform 5 parameter-tuning steps with constrained optimization\n  - Budget allocation: 30 seconds total for 560-point optimization\n- Variations:\n  - Use Nelder-Mead for unconstrained phase followed by SLSQP for constrained phase\n  - Vary noise amplitude (base:0.03 for 280, 0.01 for 560)\n  - Adjust repair method (clamp, rescale or interpolate)\n\n5. Compute Budget and Robustness\n- Dominant cost: O(n^2) operations in correlation at each resolution level\n- Time estimates: \n  - 280-point objective: ~100ms (SLSQP iterations)\n  - 560-point objective: ~500ms (due to larger kernel)\n- Budget: 50 seconds for 280-point, 50 seconds for 560-point\n- Best solution retained: Min C5 value across all optimizations\n- Recovery: Fall back to parent's solution if optimization fails\n\n6. Validation and Return Contract\n- Return only solutions satisfying:\n  1) Sum(h) \u2248 n_points/2 (within tolerance)\n  2) 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all i (\u221e clip-based)\n  3) Objective computed with `np.correlate(mode='full')*(2/n_points)`\n- Final evaluation of best solution performed\n- Returned output: (h_array, C5_score, n_points)",
